# Generator-owned files. A formatter must never rewrite these.
#
# A lockfile has exactly one owner: the package manager. Point a formatter at
# it and the two fight, with whichever ran last winning. Prettier expands
# pnpm's inline `resolution: {integrity: sha512-...}` blocks across several
# lines and flips every quoted key to double quotes; pnpm rewrites them inline
# on the next install. The result is thousands of lines of churn that hide the
# real dependency change inside it.
#
# Pass this file to Prettier with `--ignore-path`, alongside the repo's own
# `.prettierignore`. See the `@cteic/prettier-config` section of foundry's
# README for the exact script lines.
#
# Patterns resolve against the directory Prettier runs in, not this file's
# directory, and a pattern with no slash matches at any depth. So these cover
# nested workspace packages as well as the repo root.
#
# Build output (dist/, coverage/, .next/) is deliberately not here. It varies
# per repo and belongs in that repo's own .prettierignore, which composes with
# this file through a second --ignore-path.

# Node package managers
pnpm-lock.yaml
package-lock.json
npm-shrinkwrap.json
yarn.lock
bun.lock
bun.lockb
deno.lock

# Every other lockfile: Cargo.lock, composer.lock, flake.lock, poetry.lock,
# uv.lock, Gemfile.lock. Prettier parses the JSON ones, so naming them matters.
*.lock
